From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: pcs: qca-uniphy: keep the reference clock enabled

The external 2.5G PHY driven by this PCS (an RTL8221B on the IPQ5018 QDM530) is
fed by the UNIPHY reference clock, and needs it before the MDIO bus is scanned.
The PCS mac_config() that would otherwise enable it runs only after the
boot-time PHY attach - too late, and the PHY reads back missing, so the bus
scan finds nothing.

Mark the clock CLK_IS_CRITICAL so it is enabled from registration.

This is a workaround and the commit message should not pretend otherwise.
CLK_IS_CRITICAL says "never gate this clock", while the requirement is narrower:
one consumer needs it before it can be discovered. The reason there is nowhere
better to put it is that the dependency has to be met BEFORE discovery - a PHY
driver acquiring the clock in its own probe is too late, because without the
clock the PHY cannot answer the identification reads that would create and bind
that device at all. That makes it a bus-side concern, which is the shape
mdio-ipq4019.c already uses for its own controller clock (clk_prepare_enable()
in probe, before the bus is registered). Pointing that mechanism at the UNIPHY
reference clock would misdescribe the hardware - it belongs to the PHY, not to
the MDIO controller - and as of 6.18 nothing in drivers/net/mdio/ or
drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c consumes a per-PHY clock before discovery, so
there is no correct consumer to hand it to.

Worth raising upstream as a missing pre-discovery clock dependency rather than
carried as a local flag forever. Until then, dropping this makes the PHY
disappear from the bus.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/pcs/pcs-qca-uniphy.c
+++ b/drivers/net/pcs/pcs-qca-uniphy.c
@@ -220,8 +220,31 @@
 	init.parent_data = &pdata;
 	init.num_parents = 1;
 	init.ops = &qca_uniphy_refclk_ops,
-	/* always derive rate from the UNIPHY register */
-	init.flags = CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE,
+	/*
+	 * Always derive the rate from the UNIPHY register, and keep the
+	 * reference clock enabled from registration.
+	 *
+	 * The external 2.5G PHY fed by this clock (an RTL8221B on the IPQ5018
+	 * QDM530) needs it before the MDIO bus is scanned, which happens early
+	 * in boot; the PCS mac_config that would otherwise enable it runs only
+	 * after the boot-time PHY attach, too late, and the PHY reads back
+	 * missing.
+	 *
+	 * CLK_IS_CRITICAL overstates that. The requirement is "one consumer
+	 * needs this clock before it can be discovered", not "this clock must
+	 * never be gated" - but there is nowhere better to express it yet. A
+	 * PHY driver taking the clock in its own probe is too late: without the
+	 * clock the PHY cannot answer the identification reads that would
+	 * create and bind that device in the first place. The dependency has to
+	 * be satisfied BEFORE discovery, which means the bus side - the shape
+	 * mdio-ipq4019.c already uses for its own controller clock, enabling it
+	 * in probe before registering the bus. Pointing that at the UNIPHY
+	 * reference clock instead would misdescribe the hardware, since it is
+	 * the PHY's clock and not the MDIO controller's, and no generic
+	 * pre-discovery mechanism exists for a per-PHY resource. So this stays
+	 * until there is one.
+	 */
+	init.flags = CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE | CLK_IS_CRITICAL,
 
 	uniphy->ref_clk.hw.init = &init;
 	uniphy->ref_clk.uniphy = uniphy;
